WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.920
Sonic the Hedgehog is full of thrills, friendship,
and lots of high-speed action, but as it dashes

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.200
through its rapid-fire plot, it does stumble
on occasion.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:09.860
Do the highs outweigh the lows?

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.900
Hold on tight as we run through the five best
and five worst things about Sonic the Hedgehog.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.550
Sonic may be a quick moving blue alien who
can occasionally channel earth-shattering

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.939
bolts of electricity, but fortunately he's
got his good pal Tom Wachowski to keep him

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:24.480
grounded.

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:29.140
James Marsden does an excellent job balancing
out Sonic's hyperactive energy, approaching

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.040
the increasingly wild predicaments the two
of them find themselves in with a positive

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.960
attitude and a remarkably patient sense of
humor.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.899
Filling in the roles of a classic buddy movie
team, Tom acts as the straight man to Sonic's

00:00:39.899 --> 00:00:43.149
frenetic wise guy, but that doesn't mean that
Sonic gets all the laughs.

00:00:43.149 --> 00:00:45.440
"Do you have your child in that bag?"

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.440
"No.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.039
I mean yes, it's a child, but it's not mine."

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:53.719
Despite not having any kids of his own, Tom
can't help but radiate some major dad vibes,

00:00:53.719 --> 00:00:56.590
thanks not in small part to some truly cringeworthy
puns.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:00.109
At one point, when Sonic laments that he has
to leave Earth to go live on a planet made

00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:05.360
up entirely of giant mushrooms, Tom quips,
"at least you won't be the only fun guy!"

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:09.640
Between his bad jokes, his tendency to play
out full conversations with his donuts, and

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:13.640
his fatherly protectiveness of Sonic, Tom
really is the best friend an alien hedgehog

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.120
could wish for, and he brings a refreshing,
nurturing energy to the film.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.880
While Sonic the Hedgehog is at its best when
it's just letting Sonic and Tom hang out together

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:26.270
naturally, the film begins to show some signs
of strain whenever it leans into the conflict

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.260
between Tom and his sister-in-law, Rachel.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.200
Although Tom and his wife Maddie are shown
to have a mutually supportive and loving relationship,

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:36.060
as soon as Maddie goes to visit Rachel and
her young daughter JoJo, it becomes clear

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:41.020
that Rachel flat-out hates Tom, even going
so far as to tell Maddie she thinks they should

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.360
get a divorce.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:47.220
Later on, once Tom becomes a wanted fugitive,
it's a little more understandable why Rachel

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:51.729
doesn't want him in her house, but her animosity
toward him is established well before that.

00:01:51.729 --> 00:01:55.380
Perhaps there's a deleted scene somewhere
explaining her open hostility toward Tom,

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:58.320
but her reasoning isn't even hinted at in
the finished film.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:03.070
Considering that Tom is consistently portrayed
as the film's co-hero alongside Sonic, it

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:07.100
doesn't really make sense why Rachel is so
strongly opposed to him, and leads to conflict

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:08.840
that feels manufactured and forced.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:13.360
Family can be messy, especially when it comes
to in-laws, but since Maddie and Rachel are

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.800
shown to have a good relationship with each
other, it feels strange that Rachel seems

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:19.660
to have declared war on her sister's husband
for no apparent reason.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:26.450
Back in 2014, X-Men: Days of Future Past helped
moviegoing audiences realize the full comedic

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.980
potential in speedster characters when Quicksilver
zipped around a kitchen full of prison guards

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:34.400
as time -- from his perspective -- slowed
to a near standstill.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.300
Jim Croce's dreamy "Time in a Bottle" played
under the scene, serving as an excellent auditory

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.890
illustration of Quicksilver's lazy perception
of time versus everyone else's perception

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.180
of Quicksilver.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.390
Sonic has two scenes that riff on a similar
theme, when time seems to suddenly halt so

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:54.160
that Sonic can leisurely move around, repositioning
the people and objects around him to work

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.160
to his advantage.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.819
The first takes place during a brawl during
the first half of the movie, and the second

00:02:58.819 --> 00:03:01.770
during Sonic's penultimate showdown with Dr.
Robotnik.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:05.441
Neither one is quite as satisfying as the
scene in X-Men, but they're both still a lot

00:03:05.441 --> 00:03:09.380
of fun, and take great pleasure in showing
off just how awesome super speed can be.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:15.120
After Tom reluctantly teams up with Sonic,
agreeing to drive the extraterrestrial hedgehog

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:19.739
to San Francisco because of Sonic's poor sense
of direction, they stop briefly for supplies

00:03:19.739 --> 00:03:22.390
at a convenience store across from a bar.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:25.980
Predictably, while Tom is off getting road
trip snacks, Sonic becomes curious about the

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:31.489
bar, and heads inside, donning a coat, a cap,
and a pair of sunglasses as a "disguise."

00:03:31.489 --> 00:03:35.840
Tom tracks him down, and Sonic convinces him
to allow him to knock a few fun items off

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.879
his bucket list, since he plans to leave Earth
forever as soon as they reach San Francisco.

00:03:40.879 --> 00:03:45.209
Unfortunately, after a fun montage of Sonic
and Tom line dancing, throwing darts, and

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:49.629
riding an electric bull, the two get targeted
by a group of local bullies who tell them

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:52.310
that they're not welcome in the bar, for a
surprising reason.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:53.960
"We don't like your kind around here."

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:54.960
"What kind is that?"

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.280
“Hipsters."

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:00.680
The fight that breaks out following this challenge
feels a little random, but more concerning

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:02.620
is the way the film makes light of discrimintation.

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.900
It's a strange thing to make into a punchline,
and while the movie was likely trying to subvert

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.799
the obvious expectation that the bar patrons
are uncomfortable with Sonic because he's

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.060
a blue alien hedgehog, its attempt to equate
hipsters with marginalized communities falls

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:15.620
flat.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.370
All that running can really take a toll on
a hedgehog's feet and footwear, and after

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.930
Sonic becomes injured, Maddie, a veterinarian,
discovers while examining him that he's worn

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.720
large holes in both his socks and his sad,
mismatched, taped-together shoes.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:32.960
Of course, at the time Maddie notes the sorry
state of Sonic's footwear, she has a much

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:37.710
bigger concern, since Sonic is still unconscious
from an explosion after a close call with

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.860
Dr. Robotnik.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:42.960
However, little JoJo, Maddie's young niece,
looks thoughtfully at Sonic's shoes, then

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.160
runs off to her room, making it easy to guess
her intent.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.440
Sure enough, when Sonic wakes up and JoJo
returns, she presents him with a pair of bright

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:51.639
new red and white shoes.

00:04:51.639 --> 00:04:56.090
While it's definitely satisfying to see Sonic
complete his video game look, it's even more

00:04:56.090 --> 00:05:00.320
fulfilling to see a little girl show compassion
toward Sonic after he's spent the whole movie

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:02.280
desperately pining for friends.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:05.690
It's clear from the beginning of the film
that Sonic's constant chatter and exuberant

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:10.370
nature belie a deep loneliness, as he laments
that the only way for him to protect himself

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.340
is for others to not know he exists.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:17.210
In giving Sonic her shoes, JoJo not only fulfills
a practical need for him, but also an emotional

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.120
one -- she shows him that someone cares, and
that he's not alone.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:25.260
Early on in Sonic the Hedgehog, after Dr.
Robotnik forces his way into Tom's home, he

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.470
has his drones open fire on Tom and Sonic,
utterly destroying Tom and Maddie's kitchen

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:30.470
and dining room.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:35.310
As Sonic and Tom try to escape, Sonic observes
that the drones feel excessive, and he's right.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:38.800
For much of the movie, the conflict feels
off balance, as though it belongs in a much

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:40.170
more grandiose film.

00:05:40.170 --> 00:05:44.760
Yes, it's a film about an electrically charged
alien hedgehog running very, very fast, but

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:49.760
even so, all Sonic and Tom are trying to do
throughout the entire film is get to San Francisco

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.699
so they can live their lives in peace.

00:05:51.699 --> 00:05:55.530
It doesn't seem to balance out the extraordinary
lengths to which Robotnik is willing to go

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:56.160
to stop them.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.840
Part of the issue is that it's unclear what
Dr. Robotnik is even trying to do.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:04.580
He's lauded by many characters as being a
supergenius, which he'd have to be to invent

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.669
all of his machines, but his plan makes very
little sense.

00:06:07.669 --> 00:06:11.870
He says he wants to study Sonic, but attempts
to utterly annihilate him at every turn.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:15.949
Once he learns that Sonic's quills contain
an infinite supply of energy, instead of attempting

00:06:15.949 --> 00:06:20.229
to learn how to harness that energy, he throws
even more drones and guns at the problem.

00:06:20.229 --> 00:06:24.199
His actions just don't seem to jibe with his
motivations, and while the film waves this

00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:28.770
inconsistency away with a simple "he's crazy,"
it comes across as one of the weaker aspects

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.380
of a mostly competent plot.

00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:36.240
It's been a long time since audiences have
gotten to see Jim Carrey in full-on rubber-face

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.229
mode, twisting his expressions and his body
into the cartoonishly silly permutations that

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:43.520
made him one of the biggest stars in the world
in the mid-'90s.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.450
Following his Ace Ventura and Dumb and Dumber
days, Carrey took on a series of more serious

00:06:47.450 --> 00:06:52.410
roles in films like The Truman Show and Eternal
Sunshine of the Spotless Mind, which still

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:56.050
played to Carrey's comedic sensibilities,
but in much subtler ways.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:07:00.349
Now, Carrey is back with a vengeance, bringing
every bit of his bizarre '90s energy to the

00:07:00.349 --> 00:07:04.980
character of Dr. Robotnik, who chews through
scenery like a woodchipper and delivers every

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:06.330
line cranked up to eleven.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.180
"Are you in charge here?"

00:07:08.180 --> 00:07:08.980
"Yes I--"

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.000
"NOPE.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:10.560
WRONG.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:11.980
I'M IN CHARGE.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:13.440
ME.

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:14.900
I'm in charge."

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:19.760
For fans nostalgic for Carrey's most famous
roles, it's like climbing into a time machine.

00:07:19.770 --> 00:07:22.949
Watching him in Sonic the Hedgehog, it's hard
to believe that it's been more than a quarter

00:07:22.949 --> 00:07:27.300
of a century since Ace Ventura, as Carrey
channels the much younger version of himself

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:28.300
with ease.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:32.389
In one particularly delightful scene, Robotnikv
dances as he works in his lab, and the whole

00:07:32.389 --> 00:07:37.229
sequence just Carrey doing what Carrey does
best, milking the visual medium and his unique

00:07:37.229 --> 00:07:38.990
skill set for all they're worth.

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.449
“... when you see him do the things that
made you love comedy as a kid and see him

00:07:43.449 --> 00:07:45.610
like reinventing that and playing with that
again.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.180
It's just incredibly exciting."

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:52.300
For all of Carrey's comedic strengths, his
performance winds up being somewhat out of

00:07:52.300 --> 00:07:53.300
sync with the rest of the movie.

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:58.139
Carrey's over-the-top comedy doesn't entirely
gel with the easy banter between Tom and Sonic,

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:02.620
and his exaggerated physicality feels at odds
with the grounded nature of the other human

00:08:02.620 --> 00:08:03.160
characters.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.340
Granted, Carrey's entire brand in the '90s
was built around being an energetic, flamboyant

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:11.400
weirdo in a sea of everymen, and that's definitely
what he's doing here as well.

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:15.580
But that sort of character feels almost out
of place in Sonic, as though Dr. Robotnik's

00:08:15.580 --> 00:08:19.169
plot -- even though it helps drive the main
conflict of the movie -- was just stapled

00:08:19.169 --> 00:08:20.910
on from a totally different film.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:24.300
The problem may be that Carrey's playing a
villain here, not the protagonist.

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:28.690
The movie isn't built around him, and as such,
it doesn't accommodate his overwhelming presence

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:31.379
nearly as neatly as his starring vehicles
have.

00:08:31.379 --> 00:08:35.700
It worked for a different villainous role,
when Carrey played the Riddler in 1995's Batman

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.560
Forever, but Sonic lacks the wacky, neon energy
that helped Carrey's performance slot neatly

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:41.830
into the Batman world.

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:45.920
Here, although Carrey is undeniably a master
of his craft, he winds up straining the seams

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:46.480
of the film.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:51.250
To put it simply, Carrey is great, but he
may just be too much for Sonic the Hedgehog.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:52.250
"OHHHHHHH.

00:08:52.250 --> 00:08:54.100
Give me a big!

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:54.680
Fat!

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:55.420
Break!"

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:59.280
The character of Sonic may be best known for
his speed, but when it comes to the story

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:03.340
of Sonic the Hedgehog, the film has a firm
grasp on what's actually most important to

00:09:03.340 --> 00:09:06.080
the little blue guy, and it's not how fast
he can run.

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:10.540
Early on in the film, after watching a group
of kids congratulate one another after a Little

00:09:10.540 --> 00:09:14.030
League baseball game, Sonic plays a game of
baseball by himself.

00:09:14.030 --> 00:09:17.940
Using his speed, he can play every position
all at once, but after an entertaining sequence

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:22.620
of Sonic running the bases, he holds up his
hand for a high five and… no one's there.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:26.529
The speedster sequences in Sonic are a lot
of fun, but where the film really finds its

00:09:26.529 --> 00:09:29.130
heart is in its exploration of Sonic's isolation.

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:32.750
While Sonic lies to himself, pretending that
the imaginary friendships he's made up with

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:36.540
the people of Green Hills are enough to sustain
him, it's clear that he longs for connection

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:40.279
with someone outside of himself, someone who
knows and cares that he's there.

00:09:40.279 --> 00:09:44.680
After the final showdown with Robotnik, Sonic
finally gets a real high five from Tom, which

00:09:44.680 --> 00:09:48.200
feels far more satisfying than Sonic's triumph
over Robotnik.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:52.450
Ultimately, the film closes with Tom and Maddie
making Sonic his own "cave" in their house,

00:09:52.450 --> 00:09:56.310
making him part of the family and proving
with their actions that finally, Sonic has

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:58.080
a place where he truly belongs.

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:03.040
Although Sonic's ending with the Wachowskis
is nothing short of perfect, the rest of the

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.020
film's odd plot doesn't wrap up nearly so
neatly.

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:09.500
Early on in Sonic the Hedgehog, Pentagon officials
meet to investigate the mysterious blackout

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:14.300
that Sonic caused in Green Hills, and decide
to hire Dr. Robotnik to look into it, since

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.019
he's the smartest person they know.

00:10:16.019 --> 00:10:20.370
As events progress, the government frames
Tom as a terrorist, plastering his photo across

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:24.579
every major news network along with headlines
that label him a wanted fugitive.

00:10:24.579 --> 00:10:28.240
Ultimately, Sonic and Tom wind up sending
Robotnik through a portal to the mushroom

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:31.410
planet, wiping the government's asset off
the face of the earth.

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:35.930
Yet at the end of the film, Tom is back at
home, still talking about his career in law

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:39.310
enforcement, and completely unconcerned that
anyone might be looking for him.

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.370
When Commander Walters knocks on his door,
it isn't to arrest Tom or even to reprimand

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:47.690
him, but to thank Tom and Maddie for their
discretion, and give them… a $50 gift card

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:48.700
to Olive Garden?

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:52.320
Sure, paying off Tom's Olive Garden comment
from the beginning of the film is worth a

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:54.250
chuckle, but it's at the expense of all logic.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:57.351
Shouldn't the government be concerned about
what happened to Robotnik?

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.620
Shouldn't they be wanting to pin all the damage
that occurred on Tom, who they already painted

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:01.620
as a terrorist?

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.339
Do they just not care about the blackout anymore?

00:11:04.339 --> 00:11:07.950
While it's great that Sonic, Tom, and Maddie
get to live happily ever after -- at least

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:11.400
until the sequel -- it's hard to overlook
just how little sense it makes.

